<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sipsong Panna</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Chiang Hung (Jinghong)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tai Lue kingdom of the upper Mekong, centred on Chiang Hung (Jinghong), which balanced Chinese and Burmese overlords for over seven centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> chronicles date the kingdom to 1180, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaya-choeng">Phaya Choeng</a> united the Tai principalities of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and founded his capital at Chiang Hung. The later name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Sipsong Panna</a> means 'twelve thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields', referring to its administrative districts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruler, the chao phaendin or saopha, governed with a council of nobles over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> villages and surrounding hill peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akha">Akha</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a> tied it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> and the Burmese world. From the Yuan period it was a native chieftaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but it also paid tribute to Burmese kings, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a>, often to both at once.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> and Republican governments increasingly intervened, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took control in 1950. In 1953 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture</a> replaced the old monarchy; the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-mom-kham-le">Chao Mom Kham Le</a> (Dao Shixun), became an official of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sipsong-panna.jpg" alt="Sipsong Panna" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Chlukoe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinghongcity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chiang Hung (Jinghong)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Phaya Choeng, Chao Mom Kham Le (Dao Shixun)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tai Lue</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xishuangbanna, Chiang Hung, Moeng Lue, Chiang Rung</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=sipsong-panna" aria-label="Sipsong Panna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7382 1365 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sipsong Panna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7702 1621L7867 1005M7702 1621L7643 1242M7702 1621L7567 1068M7702 1621L7609 1732M7702 1621L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M7643 1242h0M7567 1068h0M7609 1732h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7702 1621h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7702 1621h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=sipsong-panna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
